{"name":"Dragon Fruit","hex":"#d75969","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"dragon fruit","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The fruit of certain cacti of the genus (Stenocereus (syn. Hylocereus) spp.), cultivated in Southeast Asia and Central and South America, having cerise-pink- or yellow-coloured skin and a white or pink sweet fleshy interior with black seeds."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dragon_fruit"}],"entries":[{"title":"Dragon fruit","blocks":[{"text":"Dragon fruit, also called pitahaya or pitaya, is the fruit of several species in the cactus genus Selenicereus, particularly Selenicereus undatus, native to the southern regions of Mexico and the Pacific coast of Guatemala, Costa Rica, and El Salvador. In the 21st century, its cultivation has expanded to regions including Vietnam, China, Colombia, and Southeast Asia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Pitaya_cross_section_ed2.jpg/330px-Pitaya_cross_section_ed2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dragon_fruit"},{"title":"Dragon","blocks":[{"text":"A dragon is a mythical creature found in the folklore of cultures throughout the world. It is roughly serpentine in form, and often possesses features drawn from different animals, such as wings and claws. In nearly all cultures, it is conceived of as a powerful and awe-inspiring creature."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/St_Catherine%2C_St_George_and_the_Dragon_%28M%C3%A4staren_fr%C3%A5n_Kappenberg%29_-_Nationalmuseum_-_18337_%28brightened%29%2C_draken.png/330px-St_Catherine%2C_St_George_and_the_Dragon_%28M%C3%A4staren_fr%C3%A5n_Kappenberg%29_-_Nationalmuseum_-_18337_%28brightened%29%2C_draken.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dragon"},{"title":"Fruit","blocks":[{"text":"In botany, a fruit is the seed-bearing structure in flowering plants (angiosperms) that is formed from the ovary after flowering."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Fruit_Platter-_Seasonal_Fruits.jpg/330px-Fruit_Platter-_Seasonal_Fruits.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fruit"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Dragonfruit_Chiayi_market.jpg/500px-Dragonfruit_Chiayi_market.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Dragonfruit_Chiayi_market.jpg/1280px-Dragonfruit_Chiayi_market.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Dragonfruit_Chiayi_market.jpg/500px-Dragonfruit_Chiayi_market.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Dragon fruit (Selenicereus undatus) in a Taiwanese market","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADragonfruit_Chiayi_market.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Yellow_pitaya.jpg/500px-Yellow_pitaya.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Yellow_pitaya.jpg/500px-Yellow_pitaya.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fruit of Selenicereus megalanthus","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AYellow_pitaya.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Pitaya_%28seedling%29.jpg/500px-Pitaya_%28seedling%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Pitaya_%28seedling%29.jpg/1280px-Pitaya_%28seedling%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb